WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:03.086
[phone ringing]

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:05.505
Hey, Dr. McT.
Oh, hey, Dr. Z.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:06.923
I was just wondering--

00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:09.509
which culture do you think
has the coolest-looking gods?

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.177
Ooh, that's
a tough question.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.430
I mean, a lot of cultures
imagine that their gods

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.057
look pretty similar to us
but with more limbs

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.892
and maybe a few
animal parts thrown in.

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:20.645
I mean, who wouldn't want
an extra animal part thrown in?

00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:22.564
I would love
to have wings.

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:24.524
That'd be tight.
The tightest.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.401
But, anyway,
I think it comes down

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:28.403
to how those gods
accessorize,

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:31.448
and for me, the gods and
goddesses of Nahua mythology

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:32.949
have always stood out.

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:34.117
I know, right?

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.286
The bold colors,
the expressive features,

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:37.579
all those feathers.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:40.248
The Nahua definitely had
some cool looking gods,

00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:41.875
but I think
aesthetics were far

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.252
from the only interesting
thing about them.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:45.545
Oh, for sure.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.338
The Nahua also have
a complicated history

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:50.133
and an even more fascinating
creation mythology.

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.426
You know what?

00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:53.803
I think we should do
an episode about this.

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:55.221
Word.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.932
[intro music]

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:02.061
If the word "Nahua"
sounds unfamiliar to you,

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.063
it's probably because
you're more used to

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:06.357
hearing them referred to
as the Aztec.

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:08.067
The history is complicated,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.903
but the name "Aztec"
was popularized

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.572
by a German explorer
in the 1800s

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:15.450
to describe the powerful
Mesoamerican Empire.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.994
It was derived from the word
Aztlan,

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:20.288
which was the name
of the legendary homeland

00:01:20.288 --> 00:01:23.041
that the people migrated from
centuries earlier.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.501
But it's probably not

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.211
what they would have
called themselves.

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:28.004
Neither Aztec nor Nahua

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:30.381
describe a single group
of people,

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:33.218
but Nahua does describe
the larger community

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:36.262
of independent city-states
who traded with each other,

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:37.806
held overlapping beliefs,

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:40.266
and spoke
the Nahuatl language.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.102
By the time the Spanish
invaded in 1521,

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:45.730
the most powerful of these
groups were the inhabitants

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.482
of the empire's
capital city,

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:50.443
Tenochtitlan,
in modern-day Mexico City.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.612
They called themselves
the Mexica,

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.240
which is where the word
"Mexico" comes from.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.908
As their influence spread--

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:58.535
not always
through peaceful means--

00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:01.621
the Mexica adopted
more than 100 local deities

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.081
into their pantheon

00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:06.084
while also inserting
their own beliefs and stories.

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:08.962
(Dr. Zarka)
Some legends say the Mexicas
began a pilgrimage

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.131
from Aztlán,
or "Northern Location,"

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:14.634
until they were urged
to leave by Huitzilopochtli,

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.386
the god of war and sacrifice.

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:18.388
He bid them to leave
their homeland

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:20.265
and travel until
they came to a place

00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:23.977
where an eagle devoured
a snake resting on a cactus.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.603
For more than 200 years,

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:27.981
they migrated south
through land much harsher

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.191
than the home
they left behind.

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.193
Weeds scratched them
as they walked

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:34.153
and rocks bit them
as they slept.

00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:37.031
But Huitzilopochtli's guidance
eventually led them

00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:40.493
to a small island
in the middle of Lake Texcoco

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.122
in the Anáhuac Valley
around 1325.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:46.249
This and most
of the other stories we know

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.751
from Nahua mythology
come from pictographic records

00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:51.170
that describe their history,
sacred rituals,

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.755
and religious beliefs.

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:55.633
The tlatoani, or chief speaker
of the empire,

00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:58.511
was said to own a whole library
of these codices

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.139
before most were destroyed
during the conquest.

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:03.683
Luckily, a few were kept safe,
and the surviving Nahua

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.560
continued to record
their history

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.605
in more codices
for generations afterwards.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:10.607
(Dr. McTier)
These codices also tell us

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:13.610
that the universe in
Nahua mythology is cyclical,

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:14.944
so that the end of the world

00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:17.155
is really
just another beginning.

00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:18.615
In the true beginning,

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:20.450
there was nothing
but an endless void

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.286
from which the dual-natured god
Ometeotl formed.

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:25.872
You may remember from
our "Gender Fluidity" episode

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.750
that Ometeotl
was both male and female,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.754
made of the god Ometecuhtli
and the goddess Omecihautl.

00:03:32.754 --> 00:03:36.591
There are several examples
of similar male-female pairs

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.092
in Nahua mythology.

00:03:38.092 --> 00:03:39.510
In this binary form,

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.721
Ometeotl produced
four sons,

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.182
Huitzilopochtli,
Quetzalcoatl,

00:03:44.182 --> 00:03:46.684
Tezcatlipoca,
and Xipe Totec.

00:03:46.684 --> 00:03:49.854
Each of them was associated
with a specific color,

00:03:49.854 --> 00:03:52.148
bird,
and cardinal direction.

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:55.068
As Ometeotl retreated
to the highest plane of heaven,

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.612
these four gods
created many others,

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.864
including the rain god,
Tlaloc.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:02.742
He and Huitzilopochtli
were the most venerated

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.202
among the Mexica,

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:06.329
and the Templo Mayor
in Tenochtitlan

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:07.914
was dedicated to them.

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:11.084
Together, the gods
created and ruled the universe,

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:14.045
which was split
into three vertical regions.

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:16.589
In the middle was the Earth,
made from the body

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.801
of the crocodile sea monster
Cipactli.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:21.135
Thirteen levels of heaven

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:22.929
stretched
above Cipactli's head,

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:26.057
and nine levels of
the underworld stretched below.

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:28.559
The Earth was dark,
so the gods created a sun

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:30.103
to keep it warm and bright.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:33.690
But making something as powerful
as the sun is no easy feat,

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:34.816
even for a god.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:36.609
It requires sacrifice,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.278
which is a central theme
in Nahua mythology.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.155
Tezcatlipoca,
a god of night

00:04:41.155 --> 00:04:42.657
who was adopted by the Nahua

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:44.867
from the indigenous
Toltec people,

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:47.412
became the sun
of the first creation,

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:49.414
but was a weak,
nocturnal sun

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:52.625
and was overthrown
by his brother, Quetzalcoatl,

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.294
a god of wind, life,
and light.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:58.506
Thus started the next cycle
of the Nahua universe,

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:01.300
the one where the first humans
were made.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.136
But the humans
became corrupt,

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:07.181
and Quetzalcoatl destroyed them
with terrifying hurricanes.

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:08.808
The few humans who survived

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.894
escaped to the tops of trees
and became monkeys.

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:14.897
(Dr. McTier)
The next sun,
or cycle of the universe,

00:05:14.897 --> 00:05:16.441
was ruled by Tlaloc,

00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:18.317
the god of rain and thunder.

00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:20.028
It was a prosperous time

00:05:20.028 --> 00:05:22.989
until it ended in a barrage
of fiery rain.

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:25.992
In some legends,
Tlaloc sent the fire himself

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.911
after Tezcatlipoca
stole his wife away.

00:05:28.911 --> 00:05:31.456
In others, it was
the ongoing feud

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:33.875
between Quetzalcoatl
and Tezcatlipoca

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:35.460
that ended the third sun.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.795
The fourth god
to sacrifice themself

00:05:37.795 --> 00:05:39.464
was Chalchiuhtlicue,

00:05:39.464 --> 00:05:42.341
Tlaloc's sister and
the goddess of running water.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.510
The feuding brothers
struck her down,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.722
and the world ended in a great
flood that collapsed the sky,

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:50.183
and all the people
were turned into fish.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:53.227
In some stories,
Tezcatlipoca taunts her

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:56.314
until she cries so many tears
that she floods the world.

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:59.317
When it came time
to create the fifth sun,

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:02.278
most of the gods were
understandably hesitant

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:03.821
to sacrifice themselves.

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:06.908
It was Nanahuatzin,
the humble, pustulous one,

00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:09.035
who jumped into the fire first.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.662
In doing so,
he created Tonatiuh,

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:15.875
who, in some stories,
ruled over the fifth sun.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.543
Not to be outdone,

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.754
Tecciztecatl
jumped in next

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:22.465
and became the moon
to Nanahuatzin's sun.

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:25.885
So that they didn't repeat
their previous mistakes,

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:28.429
the other gods
sacrificed their blood

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:31.099
to Nanahuatzin
to keep him strong.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:33.768
The world under the fifth sun
needed people,

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:36.896
so Quetzalcoatl descended
to Mictlan, the underworld,

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.565
to retrieve the bones
of the first humans.

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:42.735
Mictlantecuhtli, the lord
of this land of the dead,

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:45.321
told Quetzalcoatl
that he could have the bones

00:06:45.321 --> 00:06:47.907
only if he traveled
through all of Mictlan

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:50.451
four times
while blowing a conch shell.

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:52.954
But the shell
had no holes in it.

00:06:52.954 --> 00:06:55.248
It goes to show
that no matter the culture,

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:57.542
you can't expect
to get anything from a god

00:06:57.542 --> 00:07:00.503
without first completing
some impossible task.

00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:02.630
Clever Quetzalcoatl
called the worms

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.716
to chew holes in the shell
and filled it with bees

00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:08.386
so that their buzzing
would make the horn sing.

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:10.847
When Quetzalcoatl
carried the bones up to Earth,

00:07:10.847 --> 00:07:13.349
he ground them up
and mixed his blood

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:15.143
with the powder
to make a paste

00:07:15.143 --> 00:07:18.229
that was used like Play-Doh
to make people.

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:20.815
The Nahua believed that
they were some of these humans

00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:23.317
crafted for the fifth sun
and that this world

00:07:23.317 --> 00:07:26.279
would one day end
in a devastating earthquake.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:29.448
(Dr. Zarka)
Ritual sacrifice of humans,
animals, and food

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:31.409
was central
to Nahua culture--

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:34.453
so important that priests
supposedly sacrificed

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.831
20,000 people in one day

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:39.167
to honor the completion
of the Great Temple

00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:40.751
in Tenochtitlan.

00:07:40.751 --> 00:07:43.087
Some historians say
all these sacrifices

00:07:43.087 --> 00:07:45.506
were meant to instill fear
and obedience

00:07:45.506 --> 00:07:48.718
in the Nahua city-states
that the Mexica conquered.

00:07:48.718 --> 00:07:51.137
But according to mythology
from the codices,

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:55.474
the sacrifices were meant to
empower and appease the gods.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:58.519
Following the story
of Nanahuatzin's sacrifice,

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:00.938
according to Mexica
state ideology,

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:03.900
even the sun required
the power that blood provided

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.318
to rise each day.

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:07.361
In Nahua culture,
like in Norse,

00:08:07.361 --> 00:08:08.905
the quality of your afterlife

00:08:08.905 --> 00:08:11.657
depended not
on how morally you lived

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:13.784
but on the manner of your death.

00:08:13.784 --> 00:08:15.828
Those who died as sacrifices
to the gods

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:17.663
got the honor
of accompanying the sun

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:20.041
as it rose to its midpoint
in the sky

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:22.168
along with warriors
who died in battle.

00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:24.837
The souls of women
who died in childbirth

00:08:24.837 --> 00:08:26.672
were honored
to accompany the sun

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:29.634
as it set to the west,
where it descended to Mictlan

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:32.178
to make its journey
back to the east.

00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:34.305
After four years
of this service,

00:08:34.305 --> 00:08:37.016
the souls returned to Earth
as exotic birds--

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:39.185
a nod to the god
Huitzilopochtli

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:42.021
whose name means
"hummingbird of the south."

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:44.899
People who died from drowning
or lightning strikes

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:47.777
spent their afterlives
in Tlalocan,

00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:50.238
a paradise
in the lowest level of heaven

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:55.076
ruled by Tlaloc and his
sister-consort Chalchiuhtlicue.

00:08:55.076 --> 00:08:56.911
Everyone else spent
their afterlife traveling

00:08:56.911 --> 00:08:58.704
through the nine levels
of Mictlan.

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:02.708
For four years, the dead
faced obstacle after obstacle.

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:04.377
They climbed
an obsidian mountain,

00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:06.337
braved winds so cold
they cut the skin,

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:10.174
and evaded ferocious beasts
who feasted on human hearts.

00:09:10.174 --> 00:09:12.093
Eventually, they reached
the ninth level,

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:14.220
the home of the lord
and lady of death,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.763
where they could finally rest.

00:09:15.763 --> 00:09:17.974
But they didn't have to
make the trip alone.

00:09:17.974 --> 00:09:20.184
The souls of the dead
were guided by a white dog

00:09:20.184 --> 00:09:22.061
named Xolotl,
who, by the way,

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:24.772
is very reminiscent of Zero,
the ghostly dog

00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:26.732
in "The Nightmare
Before Christmas."

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.610
(Dr. McTier)
The Spanish destroyed
so many vestiges

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:30.945
of Nahua culture,

00:09:30.945 --> 00:09:33.239
from documents
to buildings to art,

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:35.908
but they couldn't wipe out
the culture completely.

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:38.869
When Mexico gained
its independence in 1821

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:41.747
after 300 years
of Spanish rule,

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.166
its leaders drew inspiration
from Nahua culture

00:09:44.166 --> 00:09:47.003
to establish Mexico's
national identity.

00:09:47.003 --> 00:09:50.172
You can see signs of this
in murals around Mexico City,

00:09:50.172 --> 00:09:53.592
the flag that sports the
legendary snake-chomping eagle

00:09:53.592 --> 00:09:55.845
prophesized by
Huitzilopochtli,

00:09:55.845 --> 00:09:58.180
and--oh yeah--
the name of the country

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:01.267
that's derived from the name
of the Mexica people.

00:10:01.267 --> 00:10:03.269
The culture isn't
just alive in Mexico;

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:06.564
references to Nahua mythology
can be found in graphic novels,

00:10:06.564 --> 00:10:08.816
video games,
and other media,

00:10:08.816 --> 00:10:10.693
if you know how to spot it.

00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:13.237
Nahua culture
is richly complex

00:10:13.237 --> 00:10:15.114
with stories
that reflect and explain

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:17.783
the sometimes confusing
duality of nature.

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:19.243
Beyond mythology,

00:10:19.243 --> 00:10:21.329
the Nahua had an interesting
political structure,

00:10:21.329 --> 00:10:23.080
an impressive
agricultural system,

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.958
and two calendars,
yet most people these days

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:28.419
don't even know them
by their real name.

00:10:28.419 --> 00:10:30.629
Dr. McT., I know neither of us
got to learn much

00:10:30.629 --> 00:10:32.465
about Nahua culture
as school kids,

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:34.258
but I'm pretty excited
we get to now.

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:35.843
Yeah, and I know
those little weirdos

00:10:35.843 --> 00:10:38.179
would have loved
all the bloody parts.

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:39.639
Oh, for sure.

00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:43.434
[soft music]

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:46.479
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

